WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:14.792 --> 00:00:20.480
The number one reason your startup will
fail is going to be a people issue.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.980
There has been several different
data collections on this topic, and

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.210
the consensus across different researches
is the vast majority of startups fail.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.459
One of the statistics is nine
out of ten startups fail.

00:00:32.459 --> 00:00:36.017
And Noam Wasserman in his book The
Founder's Dilemmas investigated through

00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:37.220
a series of interviews and

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.210
surveys why these startups fail
to achieve their stated goals.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:44.630
That the vast majority of those failures,
six of the nine failures were people

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:49.760
issues, there was a fight between
co-founders that torn the company asunder.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.602
It was a lack of agreement about who
was going to be the leader between two

00:00:52.602 --> 00:00:54.913
founders, that kept the company
from moving forward.

00:00:54.913 --> 00:00:57.939
It was a lack of culture that
created ongoing churn and

00:00:57.939 --> 00:01:00.168
an inability for the company to scale.

00:01:00.168 --> 00:01:02.724
If you think about it in a startup,
everything that you do,

00:01:02.724 --> 00:01:06.048
even choices of which products you want
to make or how how you're going do it,

00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:08.758
come back to people, and
the people that you have in the room and

00:01:08.758 --> 00:01:11.240
their ability to effectively
make decisions together.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.640
And this comes from things like diversity,
it comes from culture,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.939
it comes from emotional intelligence,
conflict management skills.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.187
In my mind, and again, as a psychologist
I am saying this, everything that you're

00:01:21.187 --> 00:01:23.535
going to be doing in your startup
is going to be about the people.

00:01:23.535 --> 00:01:26.892
And the sooner that you realize that and
you're as intentional about the people

00:01:26.892 --> 00:01:30.297
choices you make in your startup as you
are about the product, the more likely you

00:01:30.297 --> 00:01:32.899
are to be that one in ten startups
that actually does make it and

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:35.896
succeeds and becomes a unicorn that
the rest of the world talks about.

00:01:35.896 --> 00:01:42.798
[MUSIC]

00:01:42.798 --> 00:01:46.492
It's very competitive to find the best
talent and entrepreneurs often say well,

00:01:46.492 --> 00:01:49.870
I don't have money, I'm never
going to to be able to get A players.

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:50.520
Well, guess what?

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.360
If you create an amazing place to
work that's very mission driven,

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.330
you actually can draw in the top talent,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.970
particularly the top talent whose values
and ideas are in line with your own.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.900
And so being very intentional from the
get-go after you have your leadership in

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.140
place, idea what you want in the team.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.060
Then also put as much effort
into who we are as a company.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:07.944
What is our purpose for being?

00:02:07.944 --> 00:02:09.656
We have our mission,
that's never going to change.

00:02:09.656 --> 00:02:12.305
So that's democratizing manufacturing or

00:02:12.305 --> 00:02:16.250
bringing financial knowledge to
the masses, take your choice.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.960
And then having that vision of okay,

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.290
what's a really amazing goal we
could have five years down the road?

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:25.605
The best goals, visions, if you will, the
50% chance of success and are visceral.

00:02:25.605 --> 00:02:32.302
[MUSIC]

00:02:32.302 --> 00:02:36.032
Say if creating beauty in
the world is your top value, and

00:02:36.032 --> 00:02:39.932
you're a company that's
bringing artwork to companies.

00:02:39.932 --> 00:02:42.772
And then you're going to be looking for
how that person brings beauty into

00:02:42.772 --> 00:02:45.330
their own life and
not just how they tell you one on one.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:47.440
But what are the actual
behaviors they've done?

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.630
Did they found a nonprofit organization to
develop local artists in their community?

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.400
If you talked to their family,
were they painting at a young age?

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.180
But it means investing,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.760
not just in what people have to say to you
when you're at a pitch event, which is,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.524
I feel like, often how founders fall
into co-founding relationships.

00:03:02.524 --> 00:03:05.932
But you're going slowly enough
before you apply a co-founder title,

00:03:05.932 --> 00:03:09.679
to get to know the people in this person's
life whether it's past coworkers,

00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:13.086
even family or friends are really
going to have different points of data,

00:03:13.086 --> 00:03:14.548
if you will, on this person.

00:03:14.548 --> 00:03:16.923
That are as behavioral and
concrete as possible, and

00:03:16.923 --> 00:03:20.420
not just something somebody can say
when they're being glib over a cocktail.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.980
[MUSIC]

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:31.043
Whether you're leading a traditional
team or especially leading a startup,

00:03:31.043 --> 00:03:35.210
knowing who you are and what you
bring to the table is so important.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:38.365
A lot of people don't dare to
look within enough, to come to

00:03:38.365 --> 00:03:41.920
terms with those strengths and weaknesses,
so self awareness is everything.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.440
It's also self awareness in terms of
your values and what really drives you.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.730
Doing a startup because
you want to do a startup,

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.400
I can almost guarantee it
was never going to work.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.270
Doing a startup because there's a problem
in the world that speaks to your

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:57.050
fundamental values as a human person,
that's going to work.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.970
If you talk to venture capitalists about
the type of CEO or founder they look for,

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:04.830
one of the phrases they get back a lot is
magnetic, and anybody can be magnetic.

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.170
It depends if you found your passion.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.208
You found something in the world
that deeply means something to you.

00:04:10.208 --> 00:04:17.080
[MUSIC]

